WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.249
People like to march to their own beat, and
it turns out, so do cockatoos.

00:00:06.249 --> 00:00:10.830
Palm Cockatoos, found in northern Australia
and New Guinea, are large birds that are easily

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.610
recognized by their rosy cheeks and giant
head feathers.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.740
But as researchers at Australian National
University found,

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.080
 they have something that other animals don’t: rhythm.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.180
When male cockatoos want to show off to females,
they grab a stick or a seedpod

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.880
and start whacking a hollow tree branch.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.260
This rhythmic tapping is one of the rare examples
of maintaining a beat 

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.820
 found in the animal kingdom.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.640
Animals such as whales and other birds produce
a “song,” but these don’t have the well

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.050
defined cadence of the palm cockatoo's drumming.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.280
The use of rudimentary tools to assist in
making music

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.640
has only ever been observed in two species.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.300
The Palm Cockatoo, and humans

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.110
Not even chimpanzees, humanity’s closest
cousin, use tools to make music.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:03.960
The analysis of 18 birds and 131 songs revealed
that cockatoos use repeated elements, beat

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.720
patterns, and have rhythms unique to each
individual bird.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.360
 The cockatoo’s beat may in the future inform
us 

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.420
as to why cultures developed their own rhythms and dances,

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.990
and what possibly came before the first drum
was hit.

